WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.387
NumPy and Pandas overlap, but they are designed for somewhat different kinds of work.

00:00:05.387 --> 00:00:13.222
NumPy is especially strong for homogeneous numerical arrays, multidimensional calculations, simulations,

00:00:13.222 --> 00:00:17.312
matrix operations, and numerical inputs to models.

00:00:17.312 --> 00:00:23.787
Pandas is usually more convenient for labeled tables containing named columns, mixed data types,

00:00:23.787 --> 00:00:28.162
missing-data workflows, grouping, joining, and reporting.

00:00:28.162 --> 00:00:33.427
The important point is that many NumPy ideas carry directly into Pandas.

00:00:33.427 --> 00:00:35.502
Both tools use shape. Both support Boolean masks.

00:00:35.502 --> 00:00:41.022
Both encourage vectorized operations. Numeric dtypes still matter.

00:00:41.022 --> 00:00:45.957
So when we move into Module 10, you are not starting over.

00:00:45.957 --> 00:00:53.002
You are adding labels and table-oriented tools on top of array-oriented thinking.

00:00:53.002 --> 00:00:57.667
A DataFrame may feel more familiar because it looks like a spreadsheet,

00:00:57.667 --> 00:01:02.251
but underneath many operations are the same concepts you practiced here.
